{{Short description|Parliamentary constituency in the United Kingdom, 1885 onwards}} {{Use dmy dates|date=April 2022}} {{Infobox UK constituency main |name = High Peak |parliament = uk |image = {{maplink|frame=yes|frame-align=center|plain=yes|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/High Peak (UK Parliament constituency)}}|frame-height=200|frame-width=250}} |caption = Boundaries since 2010 |image2 = [[File:East Midlands - High Peak constituency.svg|175px|alt=Map of constituency]] |caption2 = Boundary of High Peak in the East Midlands |year = 1885 |abolished = |type = County |previous = [[North Derbyshire (UK Parliament constituency)|North Derbyshire]] |next = |electorate = 73,960 (2023) <ref>{{cite web |url=https://boundarycommissionforengland.independent.gov.uk/2023-review/the-2023-review-of-parliamentary-constituency-boundaries-in-england-volume-two-constituency-names-designations-and-composition/the-2023-review-of-parliamentary-constituency-boundaries-in-england-volume-two-constituency-names-designations-and-composition-east-midlands/#lg_high-peak-cc-73960 |title= The 2023 Review of Parliamentary Constituency Boundaries in England – Volume two: Constituency names, designations and composition – East Midlands |publisher=Boundary Commission for England |access-date=2 July 2024 |df=dmy }}</ref> |mp = [[Jon Pearce (politician)‎|Jon Pearce]] |party = [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] |region = England |county = [[Derbyshire]] |european = East Midlands |elects_howmany = One |towns = [[Buxton]], [[Glossop]], [[New Mills]] }} '''High Peak''' is a [[United Kingdom Parliament constituencies|parliamentary constituency]]{{#tag:ref|A [[county constituency]] (for the purposes of election expenses and type of returning officer)|group= n}} represented in the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|UK Parliament]] since [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024]] by [[Jon Pearce (politician)‎|Jon Pearce]] of the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]].{{#tag:ref|As with all constituencies, the constituency elects one [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP) by the [[First-past-the-post voting|first past the post]] system of election at least every five years.|group= n}}
The constituency is in north west [[Derbyshire]] and based in the heart of the [[Peak District]], including the towns of [[Buxton]], [[Glossop]] and [[New Mills]].
Since the [[1966 United Kingdom general election|1966 general election]], the seat has been somewhat of a [[bellwether]], with only three exceptions: at the [[February 1974 United Kingdom general election|February]] and [[October 1974 United Kingdom general election|October 1974]] general elections the seat was won by the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]] when the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] won the most seats nationally, and at the [[2017 United Kingdom general election|2017 general election]] when the seat was won by Labour but the Conservatives won the most seats nationally.
== Boundaries == '''1885–1918''': The Borough of Glossop, and the Sessional Divisions of Buxton, Chapel-en-le-Frith, and Glossop.
'''1918–1950''': The Boroughs of Buxton and Glossop, the Urban District of New Mills, the Rural Districts of Glossop Dale and [[Hayfield, Derbyshire|Hayfield]], and parts of the Rural Districts of Bakewell and Chapel-en-le-Frith.
'''1950–1983''': The Boroughs of Buxton and Glossop, the Urban Districts of New Mills and Whaley Bridge, and the Rural District of Chapel-en-le-Frith.
'''1983–2010''': The Borough of High Peak, and the District of West Derbyshire wards of [[Bradwell, Derbyshire|Bradwell]], [[Hathersage]] and [[Tideswell]].
'''2010–present''': The Borough of High Peak.
Following the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]], which came into effect for the [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024 general election]], the composition of the constituency is unchanged.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2023/1230/schedules/made |at=Schedule 1 Part 1 East Midlands}}</ref>
The constituency covers much of northern [[Derbyshire]] and represents most of the west of the [[Peak District]] which encircles Buxton and Glossop.<ref name=peakdistrict>{{cite web|title=Map of Peak District National Park|url=http://www.peakdistrict.gov.uk/visiting/maps/pdmap|access-date=27 October 2015}}</ref> [[Crowden, Derbyshire|Crowden]], [[Tintwistle]] and [[Woodhead, Derbyshire|Woodhead]] (formerly within the boundaries of [[Cheshire]] and in the [[Stalybridge and Hyde (UK Parliament constituency)|Stalybridge and Hyde]] constituency) were brought into the seat in the boundary changes for the [[1983 United Kingdom general election|1983 general election]]. The constituency boundaries became co-terminous with the [[High Peak Borough Council|local government district]] at the 2010 general election.
==Constituency profile== The rural [[Hope Valley, Derbyshire|Hope Valley]] and the town of [[Chapel-en-le-Frith]] have a Conservative majority, whereas the north western part of the constituency, in [[Glossop]] (especially the [[Manchester]] [[overspill estate]] of [[Gamesley]]), [[Hadfield, Derbyshire|Hadfield]] and Tintwistle, are more Labour-inclined. The largest town of [[Buxton]] is often divided between the two main parties. [[Buxton]] itself is a spa town famed for its bottled water while [[Glossop]] has had a more industrial past. Tourism is a key industry in the constituency being in the [[Peak District]], attracting visitors to its landscapes of peaks and reservoirs and other attractions such as the village of [[Castleton, Derbyshire|Castleton]] with its [[Blue John (mineral)|Blue John]] mine. The seat has considerable commuting connections by road and rail with Manchester (and the Hope Valley with [[Sheffield]]), rather than the [[East Midlands]] and [[Derby]].
==History== The seat was created in the [[Redistribution of Seats Act 1885]]. Since 1910, the seat has returned mostly [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]] MPs apart from during three periods. A [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] MP was elected for the first time in [[1966 United Kingdom general election|1966]], but was unseated at the [[1970 United Kingdom general election|next general election]]. Labour gained the seat at the [[1997 United Kingdom general election|1997 general election]] and retained it at the following two general elections during the [[Premiership of Tony Blair|Blair ministry]], but it was regained by the Conservatives at the [[2010 United Kingdom general election|2010 general election]]. It was regained by Labour at the [[2017 United Kingdom general election|2017 general election]] when [[Ruth George]] gained the seat, the first time Labour had won the High Peak seat without winning the overall general election in its history.
