'''South Holland and The Deepings''' is a [[Constituencies of the Parliament of the United Kingdom|constituency]] in [[Lincolnshire]] represented in the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|UK Parliament]] since its [[1997 United Kingdom general election|1997]] creation by [[John Hayes (British politician)|John Hayes]], a [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]].

At the [[2017 United Kingdom general election|2017 general election]], the constituency recorded a higher Conservative share of the vote than any constituency since [[1970 United Kingdom general election|1970]], with 69.9% of voters backing the party. [[South Holland, Lincolnshire|South Holland]] also delivered the second-highest "Leave" vote in the [[2016 United Kingdom European Union membership referendum|2016 referendum on the UK's membership of the European Union]] (EU). 73.6% of voters endorsed the UK's withdrawal from the EU, second only to neighbouring [[Boston, Lincolnshire|Boston]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-36616028|title=EU referendum: The result in maps and charts|date=2016-06-24|work=BBC News|access-date=2018-08-30|language=en-GB}}</ref>
At the [[2019 United Kingdom general election|2019 general election]], the Conservative majority was 62.7%, the highest of any Conservative in any constituency in any general election since [[Kensington South (UK Parliament constituency)|Kensington South]] in [[1955 United Kingdom general election|1955]].{{citation needed|date=February 2020}} Hayes took 75.9% of the vote, the third highest for the Conservatives only after [[Castle Point (UK Parliament constituency)|Castle Point]] in [[Essex]] and [[Boston and Skegness]].

==Constituency profile==
This is a largely rural seat with agriculture, bulb growing and food processing as the main economic activities.<ref>UK Polling Report https://ukpollingreport.co.uk/2015guide/southhollandandthedeepings/</ref> Incomes and house prices are below UK averages.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Seat Details - South Holland and The Deepings |url=https://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/fcgi-bin/seatdetails.py?seat=South+Holland+and+The+Deepings |access-date=2024-06-04 |website=www.electoralcalculus.co.uk}}</ref>

==Boundaries==
'''1997–2010''': The [[South Holland, Lincolnshire|District of South Holland]], with the wards of [[Deeping St James]], [[Market Deeping]], [[West Deeping]] and Truesdale, which are all in the District of [[South Kesteven]].

The constituency was created in 1997 from parts of the former seats of [[Holland with Boston (UK Parliament constituency)|Holland with Boston]] and [[Stamford and Spalding (UK Parliament constituency)|Stamford and Spalding]]. It covers the area around [[Spalding, Lincolnshire|Spalding]].  It roughly corresponds to the local government District of [[South Holland, England|South Holland]], but with [[Market Deeping]], [[Deeping St James]] and [[West Deeping]] added.

'''2010–present''': The District of South Holland, with Deeping St James, Market Deeping and West Deeping.

In minor boundary changes that were put in place for the [[2010 United Kingdom general election|2010 general election]], parts of the two [[civil parish]]es of Baston and Langtoft, forming much of the Truesdale ward, were moved to the neighbouring constituency of [[Grantham and Stamford (UK Parliament constituency)|Grantham and Stamford]]. This made [[Market Deeping|Market & West Deeping]] and [[Deeping St James]] the only [[South Kesteven]] [[civil parishes in England|parishes]] to stay in the constituency, the remainder being in [[South Holland, Lincolnshire|South Holland District]].

Following the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]], which came into effect for the [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024 general election]], the composition of the constituency is unchanged.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2023/1230/schedules/made |at=Schedule 1 Part 1 East Midlands}}</ref>

==History==
The last non-Conservative member for the predecessor main seat was Sir Herbert Butcher (served 1937–1966) who for much of that time was in the [[National Liberal Party (UK, 1931)|National Liberal Party]] set up in 1931.

The member from 1966 until 1997 was [[Richard Body]] for the main contributor seat (in later years as MP, knighted), who had previously been an MP during the Eden government and the [[Conservative Government 1957–1964|start of the Macmillan government]] for [[Billericay (UK Parliament constituency)|Billericay]], and later wrote assertive books on [[agriculture]] and on the [[Common Agricultural Policy]].

In the [[2017 United Kingdom general election|2017 general election]], the seat returned the highest vote share for the Conservatives nationally at 69.9%.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1SGkPQqosDbVL9tSX_uMNaXPGiW2z02z2fchDezlyNss/edit#gid=0|title=GE2017 - Constituency results|work=Britain Elects (Google Docs)|access-date=11 June 2017|language=en}}</ref> In the [[2019 United Kingdom general election|2019 general election]], it was an extremely safe Conservative seat. It had the largest percentage majority, third-largest absolute majority, and third-largest Conservative vote share of any seat held by the party; nationally the seat had the eleventh-largest percentage majority of any constituency.<ref>{{cite report |last1=Uberoi |first1=Elise |last2=Baker |first2=Carl |last3=Cracknell |first3=Richard |date=19 December 2019 |title=General Election 2019: results and analysis |url=http://researchbriefings.files.parliament.uk/documents/CBP-8749/CBP-8749.pdf |publisher=[[House of Commons Library]] |pages=10, 73 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200126132419/https://researchbriefings.files.parliament.uk/documents/CBP-8749/CBP-8749.pdf |url-status=live |archive-date=26 January 2020 |access-date=15 February 2020 }}</ref>

