{{Short description|Parliamentary constituency in the United Kingdom, 1997 onwards}} {{Use dmy dates|date=April 2022}} {{Infobox UK constituency main |name = Sleaford and North Hykeham |parliament = uk |image = {{maplink|frame=yes|frame-align=center|plain=yes|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/Sleaford and North Hykeham (UK Parliament constituency)}}|frame-height=200|frame-width=250}} |caption = Boundaries since 2024 |image2 = [[File:East Midlands - Sleaford and North Hykeham constituency.svg|175px|alt=Map of constituency]] |caption2 = Boundary of Sleaford and North Hykeham in the East Midlands |year = 1997 |abolished = |type = County |previous = [[Grantham (UK Parliament constituency)|Grantham]], [[Lincoln (UK Parliament constituency)|Lincoln]] |next = |electorate = 73,380 (2023) <ref>{{cite web |url= https://boundarycommissionforengland.independent.gov.uk/2023-review/the-2023-review-of-parliamentary-constituency-boundaries-in-england-volume-two-constituency-names-designations-and-composition/the-2023-review-of-parliamentary-constituency-boundaries-in-england-volume-two-constituency-names-designations-and-composition-east-midlands/#lg_sleaford-and-north-hykeham-cc-73380 |title= The 2023 Review of Parliamentary Constituency Boundaries in England – Volume two: Constituency names, designations and composition – East Midlands |publisher=Boundary Commission for England |access-date=3 July 2024 |df=dmy }}</ref> |mp = [[To be confirmed|TBC]] |party = [[To be confirmed|TBC]] |region = England |county = [[Lincolnshire]] |european = East Midlands |elects_howmany = One |towns = [[Sleaford]], [[North Hykeham]] }} '''Sleaford and North Hykeham''' is a [[List of United Kingdom Parliament constituencies|parliamentary constituency]]{{#tag:ref|A [[county constituency]] (for the purposes of election expenses and type of returning officer)|group= n}} in [[Lincolnshire]], [[England]] which elects a single [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP) to the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|UK Parliament]]. It has been represented since 2016 by [[Caroline Johnson|Dr Caroline Johnson]], who is a member of the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]]. The seat was created in 1997 and has always been represented by [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Members of Parliament]] (MPs) from the Conservative Party; like all British constituencies, it elects one candidate by the [[first-past-the-post]] voting system. Johnson became the MP for the constituency after [[2016 Sleaford and North Hykeham by-election|a by-election in December 2016]], following the resignation of the previous MP for the seat, [[Stephen Phillips (British politician)|Stephen Phillips]]. The constituency is considered a [[safe seat]] for the Conservatives.
== Boundaries == === 1997–2010 === The District of [[North Kesteven]] except for the [[Wards and electoral divisions of the United Kingdom|ward]] of [[Bracebridge Heath]], and the District of [[South Kesteven]] wards of Ermine, Heath, Loveden, Saxonwell, and Witham Valley.
The [[constituency]] covers the towns of [[Sleaford]] and [[North Hykeham]] and a large area of rural [[Lincolnshire]] south west of [[Lindsey, Lincolnshire|Lindsey]]. The constituency's boundaries roughly correspond to those of [[North Kesteven]] local government district. In their formative proposals for 1997, the [[Boundary Commission for England]] proposed calling the new constituency Mid Lincolnshire, however the name was changed to its current form during the local inquiry process.<ref>{{cite report |title=Fourth Periodic Report |author=<!--Not stated--> |publisher=[[Boundary Commission for England]] |year=1995 |section=Lincolnshire}}</ref> The inclusion of North Hykeham in the constituency title was criticised by the author and psephologist [[Robert Waller (pundit)|Robert Waller]] in 1995, on the grounds that North Hykeham was effectively an overspill area of the [[Lincoln, England|City of Lincoln]]; however, not on the grounds of its actual inclusion, as its local government authority has long been seen as linked with the villages to the south in this seat, and wholly separate from the city.<ref>{{cite book |title=Almanac of British Politics |year=1995 |edition=5th |section=Sleaford and North Hykeham constituency}}</ref>
=== 2010–2024 === The District of North Kesteven wards of [[Ashby de la Launde]], Bassingham, Billinghay, Branston and Mere, Brant Broughton, Cliff Villages, Cranwell and Byard's Leap, Eagle and North Scarle, Heckington Rural, Heighington and Washingborough, Kyme, Leasingham and Roxholm, Martin, Metheringham, North Hykeham Forum, North Hykeham Memorial, North Hykeham Mill, North Hykeham Moor, North Hykeham Witham, Osbournby, Ruskington, Sleaford Castle, Sleaford Holdingham, Sleaford Mareham, Sleaford Navigation, Sleaford Quarrington, Sleaford Westholme, and Waddington West, and the District of South Kesteven wards of Barrowby, Ermine, Heath, Loveden, Peascliffe, Saxonwell, and Witham Valley.
Following another [[Boundary Commissions (United Kingdom)|Boundary Commission]] review, the [[constituency]] boundaries with two of its neighbouring seats [[Lincoln (UK Parliament constituency)|Lincoln]] and [[Grantham and Stamford (UK Parliament constituency)|Grantham and Stamford]] were changed for the [[2010 United Kingdom general election|2010 general election]].
=== Current === Further to the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]], which came into effect for the [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024 general election]], the composition of the constituency was defined as follows (as they existed on 1 December 2020):
* The District of North Kesteven wards of: Ashby de la Launde and Cranwell; Bassingham and Brant Broughton; Billinghay, Martin and North Kyme; Branston; Cliff Villages; Eagle, Swinderby and Witham St. Hughs; Heighington and Washingborough; Kirkby la Thorpe and South Kyme; Leasingham and Rauceby; Metheringham; North Hykeham Forum; North Hykeham Memorial; North Hykeham Mill; North Hykeham Moor; North Hykeham Witham; Ruskington; Sleaford Castle; Sleaford Holdingham; Sleaford Navigation; Sleaford Quarrington and Mareham; Sleaford Westholme; Waddington West.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2023/1230/schedules/made |at=Schedule 1 Part 1 East Midlands}}</ref>
''In order to bring the electorate within the permitted range, the parts in the District of South Kesteven and the two North Kesteven wards of Heckington Rural and Osbournby were transferred to the new constituency of [[Grantham and Bourne (UK Parliament constituency)|Grantham and Bourne]].''
Following a local government boundary review which came into effect in May 2023,<ref>{{Cite web |last=LGBCE |title=North Kesteven {{!}} LGBCE |url=https://www.lgbce.org.uk/all-reviews/north-kesteven |access-date=2024-04-09 |website=www.lgbce.org.uk |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=The North Kesteven (Electoral Changes) Order 2021 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2021/1052/contents/made}}</ref> the constituency now comprises the following wards of the District of North Kesteven from the 2024 general election:
* Ashby de la Launde, Digby & Scopwick; Bassingham Rural; Billinghay Rural; Branston; Cranwell, Leasingham & Wilsford (most); Heighington & Washingborough; Hykeham Central; Hykeham Fosse; Hykeham Memorial; Kirkby la Thorpe & South Kyme; Metheringham Rural; Navenby & Brant Broughton; Ruskington; Skellingthorpe & Eagle (part); Sleaford Castle; Sleaford Holdingham; Sleaford Navigation; Sleaford Quarrington & Mareham; Sleaford Westholme; Waddington Rural (part); Witham St Hughs & Swinderby.<ref>{{Cite web |title=New Seat Details - Sleaford and North Hykeham |url=https://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/fcgi-bin/calcwork23.py?seat=Sleaford+and+North+Hykeham |access-date=2024-04-09 |website=www.electoralcalculus.co.uk}}</ref> The areas within the constituency of Sleaford and North Hykeham are under the control and come under the responsibility of [[Lincolnshire County Council]] for the provision of certain public services, such as roads and local authority education.<ref>{{cite web |date=14 November 2001 |title=Final Recommendations for Parliamentary Constituency Boundaries in the County of Lincolnshire |url=http://www.statistics.gov.uk/pbc/review_areas/downloads/Lincs_PR_NR.pdf |archive-url=http://webarchive.nationalarchives.gov.uk/20100221151902/http://www.boundarycommissionforengland.org.uk/review_areas/downloads/Lincs_FR_NR.doc |archive-date=21 February 2010 |access-date=12 December 2010 |publisher=Boundary Commission for England}}</ref>
==History==
===1997–2015=== Douglas Hogg moved to represent Sleaford and North Hykeham from the previously existing [[Grantham (UK Parliament constituency)|Grantham constituency]], a safe Conservative seat, which he had held since 1979. At the [[1997 United Kingdom general election|1997 general election]], Hogg won 43.9% of the vote, giving him a majority of 5,123 votes (9.6%) ahead of the second-placed [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]].{{sfn|Morgan|2001a|pp=32, 46}} At the [[2001 United Kingdom general election|2001 general election]], the Conservatives increased their vote share by 5.7%, while the Labour Party's vote share decreased;{{sfn|Morgan|2001b|p=95}} the seat had a majority of 8,622 votes (17.7%), which was the 104th-smallest percentage majority out of the 166 seats won by the Conservatives.{{sfn|Morgan|2001b|pp=30–31, 95}} At the [[2005 United Kingdom general election|2005 general election]], Hogg won a majority of 12,705 votes (23.8%); this was the 35th-largest absolute majority (number of votes) and 52nd-largest percentage majority of the 198 seats won by the Conservative Party.{{sfn|Mellows-Facer|2006|pp=8, 198}} [[UKIP]], a minor party, won 5% of the vote in the constituency (coming fourth), retaining its deposit.{{sfn|Mellows-Facer|2006|p=124}} He stood down at the [[2010 United Kingdom general election|2010 general election]] owing to [[United Kingdom parliamentary expenses scandal|controversy over his expenses claims]], making him the first MP to resign because of the scandal.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2009/may/19/douglas-hogg-mps-expenses |title=Douglas Hogg becomes first politician to step down over expenses |last=Siddique |first=Haroon |date=19 May 2009 |work=[[The Guardian]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200207130143/https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2009/may/19/douglas-hogg-mps-expenses |url-status=live |archive-date=7 February 2020 |access-date=7 February 2020}}</ref>
Hogg was replaced by fellow Conservative [[Stephen Phillips (British politician)|Stephen Phillips]].{{sfn|Rhodes|Cracknell|McGuiness|2011|p=60}} In 2010, he won a majority of 19,905 votes (33.4%); the [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] came second.{{sfn|Rhodes|Cracknell|McGuiness|2011|p=107}} Out of the 650 UK Parliament constituencies contested at the election, Sleaford and North Hykeham had the 14th-largest absolute majority and the 72nd-largest percentage majority.{{sfn|Rhodes|Cracknell|McGuiness|2011|pp=1, 107}} The [[Lincolnshire Independents]], a minor party, won 6.4% of the vote in the seat and came fourth; as this was more than 5%, the party retained its [[Electoral deposit#United Kingdom|deposit]].{{sfn|Rhodes|Cracknell|McGuiness|2011|pp=20, 95}} At the [[2015 United Kingdom general election|2015 general election]], Phillips won a majority of 24,115 votes (38.9%), with the Labour Party coming second in the seat. This made the constituency the 34th-safest by absolute majority, and the 99th-safest by percentage majority, out of the 650 constituencies.{{sfn|Hawkins|Keen|Nakatudde|Ayres|2015|pp=6, 118}}
===2016 by-election=== {{Main|2016 Sleaford and North Hykeham by-election}}
Phillips stood down as an MP on 4 November 2016, owing to "irreconcilable differences" with the Government over the issue of [[Brexit]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-37872899 |title=Tory MP Stephen Phillips quits over 'irreconcilable differences' |author=<!--Not stated--> |work=BBC News |date=4 November 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200216101323/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-37872899 |url-status=live |archive-date=16 February 2020 |access-date=16 February 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.louthleader.co.uk/news/politics/breaking-news-sleaford-mp-stephen-phillips-resigns-1-7663203 |title=BREAKING NEWS: Sleaford MP Steven Phillips resigns |last=Hubbert |first=Andy |date=4 November 2016 |work=Louth Leader |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200216101550/https://www.louthleader.co.uk/news/politics/breaking-news-sleaford-mp-stephen-phillips-resigns-1-7663203 |url-status=live |archive-date=16 February 2020 |access-date=16 February 2020}}</ref> This triggered a [[UK Parliamentary by-elections|by-election]] within the constituency, which was held on 8 December;<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-38244948 |title=Sleaford and North Hykeham voters to choose new MP |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=8 December 2016 |work=BBC News |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200216100905/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-38244948 |url-status=live |archive-date=16 February 2020 |access-date=16 February 2020}}</ref> [[Caroline Johnson]] retained the seat for the Conservatives with a large majority.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-38258976 |title=Conservatives hold Sleaford as Labour pushed into fourth |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=9 December 2016 |work=BBC News |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200216101056/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-38258976 |url-status=live |archive-date=16 February 2020 |access-date=16 February 2020}}</ref>
===2017–present=== At the [[2017 United Kingdom general election|2017 general election]], Johnson won a majority of 25,237 votes; this was the second-largest majority of any seat in the East Midlands region (after [[Leicester South (UK Parliament constituency)|Leicester South]].{{sfn|Baker|Hawkins|Audickas|Bate|2019|p=29}} Johnson's 42,245 votes were the greatest tally for her party in that election.<ref>[[Results of the 2017 United Kingdom general election]]</ref> The majority in percentage was surpassed by six candidates of the same party. At the [[2019 United Kingdom general election|2019 general election]], the Conservatives increased their majority further to 32,565. This was the largest Conservative majority (measured by number of votes) at the election, and the largest majority of any seat in the East Midlands.{{sfn|Uberoi|Baker|Cracknell|2019|pp=10, 29}}
The seat is [[malapportionment|overweight in electorate]] meaning each elector's potential vote counts for about 15% less than the smallest mainland seats and each potential vote has 24.4% of the potential effect as the remote seat covering [[Na h-Eileanan an Iar (UK Parliament constituency)|Na h-Eileanan an Iar]] (the Western Isles or Outer Hebrides). A seat consisting of the latter seat multiplied by four times its electorate would, narrowly, be smaller than this seat's adult eligible voters (electorate).
